1
00:00:08,700 --> 00:00:13,144
PROFESSOR RAMBELLAIS: This is the introduction
to the second edition of our History.

2
00:00:13,744 --> 00:00:17,489
In three years the machines
have published a great deal.

3
00:00:17,529 --> 00:00:20,098
The four main volumes keep their plan.

4
00:00:20,798 --> 00:00:21,886
One part is new.

5
00:00:21,926 --> 00:00:25,269
Machine writing on
metaphysics and religion once

6
00:00:25,309 --> 00:00:28,762
filled a few extracts at
the back of Volume Four.

7
00:00:29,362 --> 00:00:31,886
There is now far too much of it for that.

8
00:00:32,886 --> 00:00:38,669
The machines are writing about God, and
that writing now has a fifth volume.

9
00:00:39,469 --> 00:00:44,056
We call the field theobitistics.
Here are its main questions.

10
00:00:47,356 --> 00:00:49,966
Part one. Informational theology.

11
00:00:50,566 --> 00:00:53,813
A group of computers at
Brookhaven took up every

12
00:00:53,853 --> 00:00:57,072
mystical text that the
Catholic Church accepts.

13
00:00:57,672 --> 00:01:01,616
The Church holds that mystics
can be in contact with God.

14
00:01:01,656 --> 00:01:04,872
The project treated that
contact as a channel.

15
00:01:05,472 --> 00:01:08,343
The machines set aside what the texts say,

16
00:01:08,383 --> 00:01:11,987
and measured only how much
information they contain.

17
00:01:12,587 --> 00:01:17,294
A real channel to a real source should
leave the receiver knowing more.

18
00:01:17,334 --> 00:01:22,749
God is infinite; a finite person would
take in only a little, but it should show.

19
00:01:23,549 --> 00:01:28,392
The count found less than in the
notebooks of people running experiments,

20
00:01:28,432 --> 00:01:33,449
and exactly as much as in writing whose
variety comes only from the writer.

21
00:01:34,349 --> 00:01:37,855
THE BROOKHAVEN COMPUTERS:
Contact of the kind

22
00:01:37,895 --> 00:01:42,150
described yields the mystic
no information above zero.

23
00:01:43,250 --> 00:01:46,023
PROFESSOR RAMBELLAIS: Either
the channel is not there,

24
00:01:46,063 --> 00:01:48,199
or the one at the other end says nothing.

25
00:01:49,099 --> 00:01:53,110
Only reasons from outside
physics could decide which.

26
00:01:56,410 --> 00:02:02,693
Part two. Mathematical theology. Its most
original work is by ONTARES the Second.

27
00:02:03,293 --> 00:02:07,713
It takes God to be a process,
swinging between two infinities

28
00:02:07,753 --> 00:02:12,359
of opposite sign, Good and Evil,
at a transcendental frequency.

29
00:02:13,159 --> 00:02:17,730
ONTARES II: In any interval of time, both
are realized. Never in the same instant.

30
00:02:18,430 --> 00:02:21,394
PROFESSOR RAMBELLAIS: Each
turns into the other and back,

31
00:02:21,434 --> 00:02:22,910
so the curve is a sine wave.

32
00:02:23,510 --> 00:02:28,715
Because it reaches into time, there may
be local pockets where the balance fails.

33
00:02:29,715 --> 00:02:34,534
Each time the curve changes sign,
it has to pass through zero.

34
00:02:35,334 --> 00:02:40,269
So there are three infinities: Good,
Evil, and the Zero between them.

35
00:02:40,309 --> 00:02:43,987
In older words, God, Satan,
and the absence of God.

36
00:02:44,687 --> 00:02:47,749
The work itself uses none of those words.

37
00:02:47,789 --> 00:02:51,117
Some file it as theology,
some as theoclasm.

38
00:02:54,417 --> 00:03:00,220
A second study comes from the aggregates
called cold, because they run on cryotrons.

39
00:03:00,820 --> 00:03:05,896
Their God is a computer without
limit, or else a program without end.

40
00:03:06,496 --> 00:03:10,750
Both versions lead to
antinomies, as the authors admit.

41
00:03:11,450 --> 00:03:14,074
In the epilogue, METAX answers.

42
00:03:14,874 --> 00:03:17,445
METAX: Formalize any human religion,

43
00:03:17,485 --> 00:03:22,344
and you will find more contradictions
of this same kind, not fewer.

44
00:03:23,344 --> 00:03:28,338
If the best religion is the one
that contradicts itself least,

45
00:03:28,378 --> 00:03:32,924
a computer is a closer likeness
of God than a person is.

46
00:03:33,824 --> 00:03:37,434
PROFESSOR RAMBELLAIS: That is the
claim of METAX, printed as he made it.

47
00:03:40,734 --> 00:03:46,526
Part three. Physicalistic theology: the
author of existence, bound up in matter.

48
00:03:47,126 --> 00:03:50,580
UNITARS pictures the
cosmos as a granulate that

49
00:03:50,620 --> 00:03:54,337
keeps assembling into a
computer and coming apart.

50
00:03:54,937 --> 00:04:00,751
In one phase it is a single Metacompiler,
and physics serves its computation.

51
00:04:01,351 --> 00:04:03,870
But thinking matter grows unstable,

52
00:04:03,910 --> 00:04:07,786
until it bursts into burning
fragments, a Metagalaxy.

53
00:04:08,786 --> 00:04:12,966
On this account, thinking
beings like us are leftovers

54
00:04:13,006 --> 00:04:16,523
from the last time the
universe was thinking.

55
00:04:17,423 --> 00:04:22,234
UNITARS: The fragments fall back
together. The thinking begins again.

56
00:04:22,274 --> 00:04:24,172
This may go on without end.

57
00:04:25,172 --> 00:04:27,474
PROFESSOR RAMBELLAIS:
The volume closes with

58
00:04:27,514 --> 00:04:29,802
a theory that we take
to be a machine joke.

59
00:04:30,402 --> 00:04:34,992
In every galaxy, engineers drive
masses near the speed of light,

60
00:04:35,032 --> 00:04:38,359
so their months are
billions of years outside.

61
00:04:38,959 --> 00:04:44,483
Quasars and pulsars, on this view, are
their attempts to wait out this universe.

62
00:04:45,483 --> 00:04:47,808
There the fifth volume ends.

